/**
 * loop-continuation.ts — what a run the loop guard stopped is told, and what its
 * comment says when there is no second turn.
 *
 * # The abort
 *
 * A run that calls one tool with the same arguments, gets the same answer back and
 * does it again is stopped by atoma's loop guard. The guard is right and the ending
 * is typed: the last `atoma_runs` entry carries `ended_because: "loop"` and a
 * `loop_abort` verdict naming the rule, the call, how many times it repeated and
 * whether the answer was cut by the output cap.
 *
 * Recovering from that used to be a person's job: read the log, work out what was
 * being re-read, write a scoped instruction. The run had the facts the whole time.
 * So the same one-second-turn machinery the other two premature endings get is
 * offered here, and this is the note it is handed.
 *
 * # Why each rule is worded separately
 *
 * `identical_answers` is the one everybody means by "loop": the same answer came
 * back, so re-reading cannot produce anything new. `empty` and `identical_failures`
 * are not that -- nothing came back, or the same error came back -- and a note that
 * told the run "you already have the answer" would be describing a fact that is not
 * true of it. `cycle` is a run of calls coming round to where it started, where no
 * single answer repeated at all. `last_error` is the tool's own sentence about the
 * failure, reused rather than paraphrased.
 *
 * # Why there is no `changed` test
 *
 * `stalled-continuation.ts` refuses to ask a run that already committed, because a
 * run that did something has no announced step to recover. Here the opposite is the
 * ordinary shape: the run pushes or opens its pull request and then loops while
 * re-reading something before the report, and the extra turn is what turns that into
 * a comment a person can read. A run that handed the work on is not reachable here
 * either -- the guard fires on a tool call, and a message that calls somebody called
 * no tool.
 *
 * # The session without the field
 *
 * `loop_abort` arrives with atoma v0.6.0. A session written by an older binary
 * records `loop` and nothing else, and `loopAbortFromSession` answers `undefined` for
 * it, so that run is treated exactly as it is today.
 */
```

